================================================================================
Overrides layer: behavioural tests for add / modify / delete and graph reads
================================================================================

Context:
The existing overrides tests only check the shape and order of the emitted SQL
(the warehouse / Postgres cannot be reached from unit tests), so nothing proved
what an edit leaves in `_overrides` or what the effective graph and the asserted
graph return afterwards. The new tests execute the stores' real SQL on in-memory
SQLite doubles and assert on rows. No production code changed in this section.

Changes:
1. tests/fixtures/sqlite_graph_backends.py (new)
   - SqliteDeltaWarehouse: warehouse client double. Translates only the Spark
     constructs the stores emit (3-part names, CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW, USING
     DELTA / CLUSTER BY, LEFT ANTI JOIN, MERGE ... WHEN MATCHED THEN DELETE,
     (VALUES ...) AS v(...), current_timestamp(), DESCRIBE DETAIL). An unknown
     construct fails loudly instead of being ignored.
   - SqlitePostgres: psycopg-style cursor / transaction double (%s, ::TEXT,
     TEMP TABLE ... ON COMMIT DROP, COPY ... FROM STDIN, DELETE ... USING,
     ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, to_regclass). Transactions roll back on error.
2. tests/units/graphdb/test_overrides_behaviour.py (new, 73 tests)
   - The same scenarios run on the Delta and the Lakebase store through a small
     harness (production DDL, production views, production store methods).
   - Add: new triple (rows, author, change_id, both graphs), mapped data never
     written, already-visible triple writes nothing, idempotence, duplicates in a
     batch, visible derived triple, restoring a hidden triple, quotes and
     backslashes.
   - Delete: mapped triple hidden from both graphs, user add dropped without a
     del row, derived triple hidden from the graph only, unknown triple, twice,
     mixed batch, delete / add round trip.
   - Modify: mapped value, user-added value, subject / predicate change, merge
     onto an existing triple, chain of edits, identical triple rejected.
   - Graphs: graph vs asserted (derived rows only in the graph), every derived
     origin, edits visible on the next read, search cache marked stale.
   - Overrides tab and counters: breakdown by origin ignores user rows, derived
     count, override stats, dormant del after the source drops a triple.
   - Build and purge: rebuild keeps user edits and drops derived rows, a hidden
     triple stays hidden when the source brings it back, purge spares user rows,
     cohort partition delete only touches its own origin.
   - Missing overrides table: breakdown and derived count return zero (covers
     the Overrides tab fix of this day).
   - Delta lazy setup: reads use `_data` until the first edit, then `_graph` /
     `_asserted`; the first edit creates the table and both views; legacy
     `_inferred` rows are migrated with their origin.
3. Validation of the tests themselves: 11 deliberate regressions of the
   production SQL (restore, drop of user adds, graph / asserted views, visibility
   check on add, clear_derived) were injected one by one; each made the new suite
   fail. The production files were restored afterwards.

Known limits:
   - SQLite is not Spark or Postgres: the tests prove the set semantics of the
     statements and views, not type coercion, `digest()` generated columns,
     Delta MERGE concurrency or Lakeflow sync. Neo4j is not covered here (its
     Cypher cannot run on SQLite); it keeps the mock-based tests of
     test_neo4j_overrides.py.

